<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Sahib Ataids</h1><p class="st-meta">1275 CE – 1341 CE (approx.) · capital Afyonkarahisar</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A beylik around Afyonkarahisar founded by the family of Sahib Ata, the great Seljuk vizier and builder of Konya.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahib-ata-fakhr-al-din-ali">Sahib Ata Fakhr al-Din Ali</a> was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sultanate-of-rum">Sultanate of Rum</a> for decades under Mongol overlordship. In the 1270s his sons were granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afyonkarahisar">Karahisar</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kutahya">Kutahya</a> and nearby lands, which the family held as a semi-independent domain after Seljuk authority collapsed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahib-ata-fakhr-al-din-ali">Sahib Ata</a> was one of the greatest patrons of Seljuk architecture, building the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ince-minareli-madrasa">Ince Minareli</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasa</a> and his own mosque complex in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/konya">Konya</a> and works in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kayseri">Kayseri</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a>. His descendants ruled a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, built mosques and schools in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afyonkarahisar">Afyonkarahisar</a> and kept close ties with the Mevlevi order.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beylik">beylik</a> was squeezed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/germiyanids">Germiyanids</a>, who absorbed its lands by around 1341.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sahib-ataids.jpg" alt="Sahib Ataids" width="320" height="248"><figcaption>Image: Alexander Keith Johnston · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:W._%26_A.K._Johnston._Asia_Minor._1911_A.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1275 CE – 1341 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Afyonkarahisar</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sahib Ata Fakhr al-Din Ali</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Persian, Turkish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sahib Ata Oğulları</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1308&amp;focus=sahib-ataids" aria-label="Sahib Ataids on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5438 787 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sahib Ataids lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5758 1043L5741 1021"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5741 1021h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5758 1043h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5758 1043h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Afyonkarahisar. <a href="/atlas?year=1308&amp;focus=sahib-ataids">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
